Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// The last strategy overused group boosting on every SQL token. On Spider, the
// supplied groups contain many schema fragments that are individually plausible
// almost everywhere in the grammar, so repeated boosting caused pathological
// local loops such as `id_id_id...` and prevented EOS/closure. The traces show
// huge numbers of BoostTokenLogits calls, many examples hitting maxSteps, and
// outputs that stayed syntactically valid while semantically collapsing.
//
// This revision changes the decoding policy in three ways.
//
// 1. Keep the good part: open the constrained SQL span immediately. Spider
//    expects a single SQL query, so we still avoid wasting steps outside the
//    grammar.
//
// 2. Remove always-on boosting. The default constrained action is now the plain
//    verified ConstrainedStep helper, which masks to parser-valid SQL tokens and
//    samples without schema-group amplification. This prevents the repeated
//    self-reinforcement that produced degenerate identifier loops.
//
// 3. Use targeted recovery/regularization only when the prefix looks risky.
//    When dead-end pressure is detected, or when the previous token before a
//    separator suggests the model may keep repeating the same schema token, we
//    do one penalized constrained step against the flattened group tokens.
//    Otherwise we use plain constrained decoding. Once the parser reports a
//    complete SQL query, we close the span immediately and stop. EOS is also
//    terminal.
//
// The result is a shorter, more stable strategy: mostly ordinary grammar-guided
// SQL decoding, with occasional anti-loop penalties instead of constant boosts.
// This directly addresses the observed long runtimes, max-step failures, and
// repetitive invalid semantics.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity: In the open-span branch, OpenConstrainedSpan sets
//   insideConstrainedOut := true and currentConstrainedOut := [], and
//   parser.IsValidPrefix([]) holds by precondition. In the close branch,
//   insideConstrainedOut becomes false, so the implication is vacuous. In the
//   plain constrained branch, ConstrainedStep preserves parser validity on any
//   non-EOS step; on EOS it leaves the state unchanged. In the penalized branch,
//   PenalizedConstrainedStep guarantees the sampled token is EOS or parser-valid
//   next, and AppendConstrainedToken then extends currentConstrainedOut to a
//   valid prefix.
// suffix: OpenConstrainedSpan appends only the delimiter and resets
//   currentConstrainedOut to [], so the empty suffix relation holds. The close
//   branch makes insideConstrainedOut false, so the implication is vacuous. The
//   plain constrained branch uses ConstrainedStep, whose postcondition preserves
//   the suffix relation on non-EOS and leaves state unchanged on EOS. In the
//   penalized branch, AppendConstrainedToken appends the same token to generated
//   and currentConstrainedOut, preserving suffix equality.
// cost accounting: We keep the loop invariant cost == 0 and set cost := steps
//   after the loop. OpenConstrainedSpan, CloseConstrainedSpan, ConstrainedStep,
//   and PenalizedConstrainedStep are unit-cost helpers; AppendConstrainedToken,
//   DeadEndDetection, FlattenTokenGroups, and LastTokenBefore are non-bumping.
//   Therefore each non-breaking branch increments steps by exactly 1.
// progress bound: OpenConstrainedSpan appends exactly 1 token and we increment
//   steps by 1. CloseConstrainedSpan appends at most 1 token and we increment
//   steps by 1 before breaking. ConstrainedStep either breaks immediately on EOS
//   with unchanged state, or appends exactly 1 token while steps increases by 1.
//   PenalizedConstrainedStep either returns EOS and we break immediately, or
//   AppendConstrainedToken appends exactly 1 token while steps has already been
//   increased by 1. Hence every non-breaking branch strictly increases steps and
//   preserves |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps.
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_END
